n{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-86682/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86682"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37358/selection/52240d406af5425cf8fa247fca99a869bdafa92991c9b91e8479c6cc75e6191a" :answer/id #uuid "a77e194c-6a54-53ce-b980-0f967fe9637a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ -2048 & 1024\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37358/selection/eb390a7c1e29832698ff14ce47ce717941769643ad7fdec2a8e